The viral biocontrol agents are the **baculoviruses**, mostly of the genus **Nucleopolyhedrovirus**. They are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods.

They support the environment because (any two):
- they are **species-specific**, with **narrow-spectrum insecticidal** action;
- they have **no negative impact on plants, mammals, birds, fish** or even on **non-target insects**;
- so they help **conserve beneficial insects**;
- they are therefore desirable in **integrated pest management (IPM)** programmes and when an **ecologically sensitive area** is being treated.

(Strictly, baculoviruses attack the insect pests of plants rather than plant pathogens; the question uses "plant pathogens" for the pests that harm crops.)

When a region loses its biodiversity, the following consequences are seen (any three):
- **decline in plant production** (and so a decline in the flora and fauna it supports);
- **lowered resistance to environmental perturbations** such as drought;
- **increased variability in certain ecosystem processes** such as plant productivity, water use, and pest and disease cycles.
